1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,177
Host: Welcome to KC desk note. Today we are unpacking one fresh research report and the question it raises.

2
00:00:06,537 --> 00:00:16,521
Host: Welcome back to the show. Today we’re looking at a fresh projection for the USD/CNY fix from Nomura’s Asia FX team. EN_B: That’s right.

3
00:00:16,881 --> 00:00:28,770
Host: The model is pointing to a significantly lower fix compared to the previous one—about 470 pips lower. EN_A: That’s a big move. What’s driving that change?

4
00:00:29,130 --> 00:00:39,068
Host: EN_B: The main factors come from a basket of currencies. The Korean won, the euro, and the Australian dollar are all contributing to a weaker dollar against the yuan.

5
00:00:39,428 --> 00:00:45,326
Host: EN_A: So it’s not just one currency. It’s a broad-based shift. EN_B: Exactly.

6
00:00:45,686 --> 00:00:55,810
Host: The top four weighted contributions include the won, the euro, the Aussie dollar, and even the Russian ruble. That tells us the model is picking up on a global trend.

7
00:00:56,170 --> 00:01:02,160
Host: EN_A: And the model also includes something called a counter-cyclical factor. What does that do?

8
00:01:02,520 --> 00:01:09,068
Host: EN_B: The counter-cyclical factor is a tool the People’s Bank of China uses to smooth out volatility.

9
00:01:09,428 --> 00:01:17,045
Host: When you add it, the projected fix is a bit higher—about 339 pips lower than the previous fix instead of 470.

10
00:01:17,405 --> 00:01:26,135
Host: EN_A: So the central bank is essentially signaling they want to slow down the pace of yuan appreciation. EN_B: That’s the idea.

11
00:01:26,495 --> 00:01:38,198
Host: It’s a way to manage expectations and avoid a one-way bet on the currency. EN_A: The report also has a chart showing model errors over time. Some of those errors are quite large.

12
00:01:38,558 --> 00:01:50,911
Host: EN_B: Yes, the model isn’t perfect. In January 2025, the error was minus 1,800 pips. That’s a big miss. But by mid-2025, it was closer to zero.

13
00:01:51,271 --> 00:01:59,770
Host: EN_A: So the model has been more accurate recently. That gives us more confidence in this projection. EN_B: It does.

14
00:02:00,130 --> 00:02:05,656
Host: But we should note that the model can still be off, especially around major events or policy shifts.

15
00:02:06,016 --> 00:02:13,679
Host: EN_A: Speaking of events, the report includes a calendar of key dates for China later this year. EN_B: Right.

16
00:02:14,039 --> 00:02:23,559
Host: There’s the Politburo meeting in late July, the APEC summit in Shenzhen in November, and the Central Economic Work Conference in December. These could all influence the fix.

17
00:02:23,919 --> 00:02:32,185
Host: EN_A: And there’s also a potential visit by President Xi to the US towards the end of the year. EN_B: That’s a big one.

18
00:02:32,545 --> 00:02:41,415
Host: Trade tensions and diplomatic meetings can have a direct impact on currency policy. EN_A: So what’s the one thing the report doesn’t fully settle?

19
00:02:41,775 --> 00:02:52,317
Host: EN_B: It doesn’t tell us exactly how the PBOC will adjust the counter-cyclical factor in real time. That’s a judgment call that depends on market conditions and policy goals.

20
00:02:52,677 --> 00:03:03,869
Host: EN_A: So the full report has more nuance on that? EN_B: Absolutely. For anyone following the yuan closely, the full analysis dives into the model mechanics and the historical context.

21
00:03:04,229 --> 00:03:13,517
Host: It’s worth a read. EN_A: Great. We’ll leave that for our listeners to explore on their own. Thanks for breaking it down. EN_B: My pleasure.

22
00:03:13,877 --> 00:03:16,199
Host: Let’s keep the conversation going in the community.
